From f12641c66c8a028e9ca25d6efd08dde9ca947519 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 03:25:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/24] J604 local build: turn the keyboard backlight off after
 10 idle seconds

macOS turns a MacBook's keyboard backlight off after a few seconds without
input and back on at the next key or touch. The platform root now names
that default for the runtime's idle.keyboardBacklight timeout: 10 seconds.
It is data, like audio.json; a user's shell.json value overrides it and 0
turns it off.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 omarchy-mac/README.md                        | 1 +
 omarchy-mac/share/omarchy-platform/idle.json | 3 +++
 omarchy-mac/test/desktop-setup-test.sh       | 4 +++-
 3 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 omarchy-mac/share/omarchy-platform/idle.json

diff --git a/omarchy-mac/README.md b/omarchy-mac/README.md
index 80bfd67..38d8ea0 100644
--- a/omarchy-mac/README.md
+++ b/omarchy-mac/README.md
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@ The Mac's hardware data lives in the platform root, `/usr/share/omarchy-platform
 - `key-names`: the keybindings menu shows `XF86MonBrightnessUp` and `Down` as the F2 and F1 they are.
 - `display-cutouts.json`: the notch of each MacBook panel (the 14" Pro measured at 64 physical rows, the 16" Pro inferred at 64 and the Airs at 56), which the bar keeps clear of. A notched panel not listed gets no floor until the file lists it; `[bar] notch-height` in `shell.toml` only adjusts the floor of a listed panel. An entry may also name `cutoutWidth`, a centered notch's measured width in physical columns. Support for placing center status content beside that notch remains proposed for the canonical desktop in [omacom/omarchy#17744](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/pull/17744); this schema alone does not change placement. A runtime without that support, or a panel without the key, keeps its current layout.
 - `displays.conf`: `omarchy-hw-display` never picks the Touch Bar's `display-pipe` or `228600000.dsi.0` backlight and prefers the Retina panel's `apple-panel-bl` (no GPU backlight exists), and `omarchy-brightness-display` probes an external monitor over DDC only when its connector has a `ddc` node, since Apple's display driver registers no DDC channel and a probe would walk the SoC's SMBus for about 3 s.
+- `idle.json`: the keyboard backlight goes off after 10 seconds without keyboard or trackpad input and comes back at its level on the next input, as on macOS (the runtime's `idle.keyboardBacklight`, which a user's `shell.json` overrides; `0` turns it off). A runtime older than the setting ignores it.
 - `audio.json`: the audio panel and microphone widget leave out asahi-audio's DSP internals: the speaker graph's output stream into the raw speakers (`effect_output.jNNN-convolver`, and the desktops' `mini`/`studio`), the microphone graph's always-running capture of the raw array (`audio_effect.jNNN-mic`), the raw devices asahi-audio marks do-not-use (`RawSpeakers`, `RawMics`), the mapper's own capture of the DSP microphone (`input.omarchy_asahi_mic`), which would otherwise count as an app recording, and the mono DSP microphone while the mapper's stereo `omarchy_asahi_mic` exists. A runtime older than the platform root reads no audio hints and keeps its own Apple checks.
 - `fingerprint-readers`: Touch ID. `omarchy-hw-fingerprint` finds the sensor once `/run/omarchy-mac/touchid` reads `ready`, or `available` before the owner has turned it on. Nothing loads the Secure Enclave driver until the owner agrees. At boot, `omarchy-mac-touchid-status.service` (`lib/touchid-status`, pulled in by the package's `multi-user.target.wants` link, before the greeter and `fprintd`) asks `lib/touchid-eligibility`, which reads only the device tree and installed files: `available` needs a sensor node, a board the aurora driver has a profile for (M2, M2 Pro and Max, the M3 Air J613 and J615, the MacBook Neo, and the 16-inch M4 Pro J616s and M4 Max J616c, whose sensor sits at the root of the tree rather than under `/soc`; M1-family Macs wait for their xART guard path), `/chosen/apfs-preboot-uuid`, a way to the calibration (under the name the sensor's `firmware-name` gives, `apple/mesacal-<board>.bin`, or `apple/mesa_calibration.bin` on M2 Pro and Max) and, on the Neo, the J616s and the J616c, a way to the sensor root key (the second `firmware-name` entry, or `apple/msrk-j700.bin`), an installed one or the factory records on the ESP with `python` to rebuild it from; the J616s's and J616c's calibration is rebuilt the same way (the extractors need `python`, which linux-aurora only suggests), and an Omarchy whose `omarchy-lifecycle-dispatch` resolves `enable-fingerprint`, since the invitation comes once and an older setup would enrol a reader nothing turned on; anything else records `unsupported:<reason>`, which no detector line names. Omarchy's fingerprint setup then runs `omarchy-lifecycle-dispatch enable-fingerprint` (`entrypoints/enable-fingerprint`, as root) before enrolling. With no status file (a Mac without a sensor records nothing, and a status service can fail) it works eligibility out again. Where Touch ID can't be set up it lets the setup go on, so a USB reader enrols on a Mac mini, a Studio or an M1 Mac as it would without Touch ID. A Mac whose owner already agreed is never asked again: it gets any missing options and, unless Touch ID reads `ready`, is told to restart, and the setup goes on. It stops the setup only when the owner says no, or turning Touch ID on or writing its options fails. It says in three lines that Touch ID is experimental and can be unstable, that it shares the Secure Enclave with macOS so macOS should be backed up first, and that the Mac manual has the details, then asks. The manual's Encryption and passwords page carries the rest: the key store Linux creates stays, the enclave's anti-replay state moves forward and is one file in the iBoot System Container that macOS updates too, and a bug that writes it wrongly can cost macOS data (with FileVault on, macOS won't start and the Mac needs a DFU restore; with FileVault off, macOS resets its keychain and fingerprints). FileVault's state isn't checked: the owner can change it after installing, so the manual names both cases. On yes, `lib/touchid-enable` carves what is missing from this Mac's own iBoot System Container into `/usr/lib/firmware/updates` (the calibration with linux-aurora's `extract-mesa-calibration`, except on the Neo, whose installer writes the sealed form, and the J616s and J616c, whose `apple/mesacal-j616s.bin` and `apple/mesacal-j616c.bin` are resealed from their `FSC2` factory records with `lib/touchid-root-key --record FSC2`; the sensor root key, `apple/msrk-j700.bin` on the Neo and `apple/msrk-j616s.bin` and `apple/msrk-j616c.bin` on the J616s and J616c, root-only `0600`, rebuilt with `lib/touchid-root-key`, which takes the SoC the seal record must name with `--chip`), records the agreement in `/etc/omarchy-mac/touchid-on` and in the driver's options (`/etc/modprobe.d/omarchy-mac-touchid.conf`: `xart_writes=1 provision_keybag=1`, today's defaults, which a kernel that loads `apple_sep` read-only by default, omacom/linux-aurora#180, writes only with; the name sorts after `apple-sep.conf`, so the owner's agreement wins over any `apple-sep.conf`, while `apple_sep.xart_writes=0` on the kernel command line still wins over both, and no initramfs hook includes `apple_sep`, so only the root's copy of the options is read), loads `apple-sep` now (a driver already running keeps the options it loaded with, so on a kernel that loads it read-only, and a Mac with no key store yet, the agreement stands and Touch ID turns on after a restart), and succeeds only once the activation records `ready`, restarting an `fprintd` that started without the reader; a failure after the record is written removes it again, so the next boot offers Touch ID again instead of loading a driver that never came up. A driver that loaded but never came up stays that way until a restart, so the status then reads `failed` and setup doesn't offer Touch ID again that boot; when `/dev/sep-bio` or the keybag never appeared, the activation never ran, and the error points to `journalctl -k`, the driver's own log. While the record exists, `omarchy-mac-touchid-load.service` (pulled in by a `multi-user.target.wants` link) loads `apple-sep` at every boot once `udevadm wait` sees `/dev/disk/by-partlabel/iBootSystemContainer`, since the driver opens that link as it probes and does not retry, and before the greeter and `fprintd`. The wait is part of the service rather than a device dependency, because systemd waits for a unit's devices before it checks its condition: a dependency would hold up every boot, agreed or not, and the greeter on a machine without the partition until the device timed out. A key store from an earlier load (`/var/lib/aurora-sep-keybag.bin`) is not agreement: that owner never saw this text, so they are asked too, told that the existing key store is used. `omarchy-lifecycle-dispatch disable-fingerprint`, after the fingerprints are deleted, removes the record and the options, so the driver stops loading from the next boot and the status offers Touch ID again; the key store stays. A record or options that can't be written, or removed, fails the operation rather than reporting success, and a carved calibration, like the sensor key, takes its name only once it is whole. On a kernel with omacom/linux-aurora#180, udev loads `apple_sep` on every Mac, read-only unless the options file exists, and a read-only driver always reports `matching` as `blocked`. The activation runs only on the owner's record, so a Mac whose owner hasn't agreed, or turned Touch ID off, keeps the boot's `available` and is offered Touch ID; and `enable-fingerprint` writes the options for a Mac agreed before they existed whatever its status, so one that reads `blocked` reaches `ready` after a restart. The Neo's, the J616s's and the J616c's sensor keys (`apple/msrk-j700.bin`, `apple/msrk-j616s.bin`, `apple/msrk-j616c.bin`) and the J616s's and J616c's calibration (`apple/mesacal-j616s.bin`, `apple/mesacal-j616c.bin`) are device-private, so nothing reads them before the owner agrees. asahi-installer copies this Mac's factory records (`com.apple.factorydata`) into the ESP's fallback firmware archive, `asahi/all_firmware.tar.gz`, on every install; an image install mounts the ESP at `/boot/efi` with `umask=0077`, so only root can read it there, and that mount is where the archive is read; on yes, `lib/touchid-root-key` (Python's standard library only) reads the `MSRk` record (`FSC2` for the J616s's calibration), the `seal` record, which must name the Mac's SoC, and the trust bundle from it in memory, rebuilds the file as omarchy-mac-installer's `factory_root_key()` does on macOS (the `MSRk` record's `IM4P` under the FDR sealing manifest, with the trust bundle as `secb`), checks its envelope, and refuses records that aren't all found byte for byte in this Mac's own iBoot System Container, so an archive from another Mac, or one swapped on the ESP, gives no file. It writes the file root-only (`0600`) under a temporary name, synced, then renames it. Nothing is kept when the owner says no, and a yes that then fails to turn Touch ID on takes back the key, and on the J616s and J616c the calibration, it rebuilt along with the agreement; `disable-fingerprint` deletes both from `/usr/lib/firmware/updates`, and the next yes rebuilds them. A factory reset gives the factory root no key, since the ESP survives it and the next owner's yes rebuilds it. Every Neo, J616s and J616c install has the archive, since upstream asahi-installer writes it on every install, so none needs the installer to collect the key. A Neo without the archive (removed by hand) or without `python` reads `unsupported:no-root-key`, and a J616s or J616c reads `unsupported:no-calibration` unless its calibration is already installed; the boot status looks for the archive, so `omarchy-mac-touchid-status.service` runs after `boot-efi.mount`. Carved files under `/usr/lib/firmware/updates` belong to no package, so a root rollback can remove them. An agreed Mac isn't asked again, so its setup doesn't carve them back: turning Touch ID off (Omarchy's fingerprint removal) and on again does. The two operations, and `available`, need the matching Omarchy change before this file ships. `omarchy-mac-touchid-activate.service` (`lib/touchid-activate`) runs only on the owner's record, so it never opens a driver something else loaded without agreement (fprintd still opens `/dev/sep-bio` when it probes its readers, so on a kernel with omacom/linux-aurora#180, which loads the driver on every Mac, fprintd's allowance for the node needs the same gate first), and writes `/run/omarchy-mac/touchid`, `0644` so the session user can read it, and only when all four of the Secure Enclave driver's `diag` attributes say Touch ID can enrol and match, as the driver's ABI document asks: `touchid` reads `ready`, `sensor_attach` reads `attached`, `restore` reads `restored` or `empty`, and `matching` reads `usable` or `unverified`. `diag/touchid` alone can read `ready` while the sensor is uncalibrated, the enrolled fingerprints are unproven, or the xART guard blocks a match, as on a cold-booting M1-family Mac, and the invitation that follows comes only once. Anything else writes the reason instead (`failed`, `not-ready`, `unproven` or `blocked`) and says why in the unit's journal. Touch ID activates only on the first open of `/dev/sep-bio`, so `94-omarchy-mac-touchid.rules` starts the unit, which opens it once, read-only, as soon as the kernel publishes it; nothing needs enabling. The kernel activates Touch ID inside that open, and activation lasts the rest of the boot after the close. So does a failed activation, so the unit opens the node only once the kernel has made this Mac's keybag, `/var/lib/aurora-sep-keybag.bin`. The node takes one opener at a time, so a node `fprintd` holds is already activated. The attach retries once about 10 s after a short fall, so the script waits until 45 s after it starts for a settled answer, and `TimeoutStartSec=60s` bounds an open that blocks. A missing attribute ends it at once. An update runs it again, and a `ready` from earlier in the boot stands while `sensor_attach` reads `attached`, unless a final failure (`failed`, `unproven` or `blocked`) appears, since `diag/touchid` follows every later bring-up and one can fall short and recover. On the install or update that brings the rule, `/dev/sep-bio` already exists and no add event comes, and the boot-time status unit hasn't run on the update that first installs it, so `90-omarchy-mac-touchid.hook` starts the status unit and then the activation after the transaction, and the same session's post-update check sees the result. On a Mac with no agreement, that status run records `available` and the activation is skipped, whatever loaded the driver, unless Touch ID failed to come up earlier that boot: a reason that failure recorded (`failed`, `not-ready`, `unproven` or `blocked`) stands until a restart, and setup says to restart. The hook is skipped in a chroot, which includes `pacman --root`, or an unbooted root, and never fails the transaction: a unit that fails says why in its journal. The post-update fingerprint invitation then reaches the owner, and the fingerprint setup installs `libfprint-git`, whose aarch64 build carries the Touch ID driver (omacom/omarchy-pkgs#806) and owns `fprintd`'s allowance for `/dev/sep-bio`. systemd resolves that allowance when `fprintd` starts, so the drop-in `fprintd.service.d/50-omarchy-mac-touchid.conf` orders `fprintd` after `omarchy-mac-touchid-wait.service`, which follows the boot-time load, waits up to 15 s for the node where the Secure Enclave driver has a device and returns at once on a Mac without one, and after the activation, so `fprintd` never takes the node while the activation holds it: when both start together, at boot or from the pacman hook, `fprintd` waits for the activation, up to 60 s on a Mac whose attach is still settling. It adds no allowance of its own, and it's inert until Omarchy's setup installs `fprintd`. No sleep hook: the kernel ends a capture before suspend, and on a MacBook Neo the lock screen took the finger on the first touch after resume. Ship this file only once a boot without `aurora-touchid` shows enrolment, verification and suspend working, which still needs: Omarchy's `enable-fingerprint` and `disable-fingerprint` operations and their calls from the fingerprint setup and removal, since `apple-sep` is a module (`APPLE_SEP=m`: built in, it probes before udev has made the iBoot System Container's partition link and fails without retrying) with no modalias, which loads only on the owner's record; and m1n1 that forwards `/chosen/apfs-preboot-uuid`, which selects this install's own lane in the Secure Enclave. Keep that source stable: changing it (boot entries with and without `apple_sep.os_uuid_hi`/`os_uuid_lo`, or a stub reinstall) orphans the Linux enrolments. Without all of that the invitation, which comes once, leads to a setup that cannot finish. The script follows the aurora kernel's naming (the `apple_sep` driver and its `diag` attributes): a kernel that renames them stops the invitation without an error, so move the script together with the kernel pin.
 - Touch ID's re-arm: on the 7.3 aurora kernel, five failed matches lock Touch ID until a verified password calls `SEP_BIO_REARM`, and the lock outlasts a restart. aurora-touchid-rearm (omarchy-pkgs) ships `pam_aurora_touchid.so` and a root-only, socket-activated helper that checks the password again before re-arming; the drop-in `aurora-touchid-rearm@.service.d/50-omarchy-mac-touchid.conf` lets it run only on the owner's record and a kernel with `/dev/sep-bio-rearm`. While `/etc/omarchy-mac/touchid-on` and the module both exist, `lib/touchid-pam` keeps a managed block (a comment line, then `-auth optional pam_aurora_touchid.so`) straight after the `auth include system-auth` line of `/etc/pam.d/sudo` and `/etc/pam.d/polkit-1`, and only where that include is the stack's single and last auth line, so no factor or jump follows it. Every run checks that placement, as Linux-PAM reads the stack (indented lines, the type in any case), and takes out a block that auth lines now follow. Otherwise, or with `--remove`, it takes out that block and nothing else. It reads the record under its lock, leaves a symlinked stack alone, and renames a new stack into place only when the text has the expected line count and the stack hasn't changed since it was read. `touchid-enable` and `enable-fingerprint` run it once Touch ID is on, `disable-fingerprint` after removing the record, `omarchy-mac-touchid-pam.service` at every boot before logins open (Omarchy writes polkit's stack after enrolling, so polkit gets the block from the next boot), `91-omarchy-mac-touchid-pam.hook` after the update that brings it or the module, and `91-omarchy-mac-touchid-pam-remove.hook` with `--remove` before either is removed, stopping the removal if that fails. Omarchy's own edits of those stacks take no lock; nothing here runs during its fingerprint setup, and the check before the rename narrows what's left. The lock screen's stack, `omarchy-lock-password`, isn't covered: Omarchy rewrites it whole and its PAM runs as the user, which the helper refuses.
diff --git a/omarchy-mac/share/omarchy-platform/idle.json b/omarchy-mac/share/omarchy-platform/idle.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1f7b29c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/omarchy-mac/share/omarchy-platform/idle.json
@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
+{
+  "keyboardBacklight": 10
+}
diff --git a/omarchy-mac/test/desktop-setup-test.sh b/omarchy-mac/test/desktop-setup-test.sh
index f723069..dbf6eb4 100644
--- a/omarchy-mac/test/desktop-setup-test.sh
+++ b/omarchy-mac/test/desktop-setup-test.sh
@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@ for entry in setup-system setup-user; do
     fail "the $entry entrypoint is staged for omarchy-lifecycle-dispatch"
 done
 platform=$stage/usr/share/omarchy-platform
-for file in key-names display-cutouts.json displays.conf keyrings audio.json fingerprint-readers; do
+for file in key-names display-cutouts.json displays.conf keyrings audio.json fingerprint-readers idle.json; do
   [[ -f $platform/$file && ! -L $platform/$file ]] || fail "$file is staged in the platform root"
 done
 [[ $(grep -v '^#' "$platform/keyrings") == asahi-alarm-keyring ]] ||
@@ -29,6 +29,8 @@ for panel in json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))["panels"]:
     if width is not None and not (type(width) is int and 0 < width < panel["width"]):
         sys.exit(f"bad cutoutWidth: {panel}")
 PY
+[[ $(python3 -c 'import json, sys; print(json.dumps(json.load(open(sys.argv[1])), sort_keys=True))' "$platform/idle.json") == '{"keyboardBacklight": 10}' ]] ||
+  fail 'the platform names a 10 second keyboard-backlight idle timeout, and nothing else'
 [[ ! -e $stage/usr/share/omarchy ]] || fail "nothing is staged in the omarchy package's tree"
 # displays.conf follows the runtime's grammar (docs/file-layout.md): one
 # directive per line, a name with no "/", comments on whole lines only. Nothing
